      "readerEdit": [
        "This gentleman had a great deal of leisure, almost the whole year, and he poured that leisure into books of chivalry. These were adventure romances about wandering knights, giants, enchantments, duels, courtly love, and impossible feats. He read them with such appetite that he neglected hunting and even the management of his property. The obsession became expensive enough that he sold pieces of farmland to buy more romances. His favorite writer was Feliciano de Silva, not because the prose was clear in any normal sense, but because the gentleman loved its tangled cleverness. Cervantes gives examples of romance language that is almost comic nonsense: phrases where \"reason\" fights \"unreason,\" or the heavens \"divinely\" fortify a lady with stars. The point is not that Don Quixote understands these sentences deeply. The point is that he treats their fog as profundity. He lies awake trying to squeeze meaning out of them, and the narrator jokes that even Aristotle, raised from the dead for this one job, could not have extracted sense from them.",
        "His reading becomes active, not passive. He worries about continuity errors and physical plausibility inside the romances. When Don Belianis suffers and gives so many wounds, Don Quixote wonders why the knight's face and body are not covered with scars, no matter how good his surgeons were. He admires an author who ends a book by promising an endless adventure, and he is tempted to finish the sequel himself. This matters because Don Quixote is not simply reading for entertainment anymore. He is beginning to collaborate with the fictional world, repair it, extend it, and treat it like a reality that needs his participation."
      ],
